        "context": "section",
        "id": "_basic_usage",
        "level": 1,
        "roles": [],
        "title": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "Basic usage"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "AsciiDoc is the language."
              },
              {
                "kind": "line-break"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " Asciidoctor is the processor."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          },
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "You compose documents using the "
              },
              {
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "AsciiDoc language"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "xref",
                "target": "asciidoc::index.html"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": ", which is a text-based writing format. The AsciiDoc language was designed to be unobtrusive and concise to simplify writing and make it more pleasant. But AsciiDoc itself is not a publishing format. It’s more like a shorthand. That’s where an AsciiDoc processor comes in."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          },
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "An AsciiDoc processor, such as Asciidoctor, reads the AsciiDoc source and converts it to publishable formats such as HTML 5 or PDF. It can also convert it to formats which themselves can be processed by a publishing toolchain, such as DocBook."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          },
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Since AsciiDoc is not published as is, the processing step provides an opportunity to augment the document by expanding shorthand codes, layering in integrations, and applying a pleasant visual style. That augmentation is a large part of what Asciidoctor does."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          },
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "In brief, you give AsciiDoc source to Asciidoctor and it gives you a result you can publish."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          }
        ],
        "context": "section",
        "id": "_relationship_to_asciidoc",
        "level": 1,
        "roles": [],
        "title": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "Relationship to AsciiDoc"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "When we talk about Asciidoctor in this section, we are talking about the core processor and built-in converters. Asciidoctor also has an ecosystem of extensions, converters, build plugins, and tools to help you author and publish content written in AsciiDoc. You can find the documentation for these projects elsewhere on this site or in the Asciidoctor organization on GitHub."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          }
        ],
        "context": "section",
        "id": "_ecosystem",
        "level": 1,
        "roles": [],
        "title": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "Ecosystem"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Although Asciidoctor is written in Ruby, it does not mean you need Ruby to use it. You can also run Asciidoctor using a JVM via AsciidoctorJ or JavaScript via Asciidoctor.js."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          },
          {
            "blocks": [
              {
                "context": "paragraph",
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "Thanks to "
                  },
                  {
                    "inlines": [
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "value": "AsciidoctorJ"
                      }
                    ],
                    "kind": "xref",
                    "target": "asciidoctorj::index.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": ", Asciidoctor can be used in any program that runs on a Java Virtual Machine (JVM). Under the covers, AsciidoctorJ uses JRuby to run Asciidoctor, but this is hidden behind a Java API. AsciidoctorJ even provides an alternate CLI, which means you can get the full Asciidoctor experience with only a Java runtime. Building atop AsciidoctorJ, there are also plugins that integrate Asciidoctor into Apache Maven, Gradle, or Javadoc builds."
                  }
                ],
                "roles": [],
                "title": null
              }
            ],
            "context": "section",
            "id": "_java_jvm",
            "level": 2,
            "roles": [],
            "title": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Java / JVM"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "blocks": [
              {
                "context": "paragraph",
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "Thanks to "
                  },
                  {
                    "inlines": [
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "value": "Asciidoctor.js"
                      }
                    ],
                    "kind": "xref",
                    "target": "asciidoctor.js::index.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": ", Asciidoctor can also be used in JavaScript. The Asciidoctor.js project uses Opal to transpile the Ruby source in Asciidoctor to JavaScript. The result is a fully-functional version of Asciidoctor that works on any JavaScript runtime, such as a modern browser or Node.js. Asciidoctor.js even provides an alternate CLI, which means you can get the full Asciidoctor experience with only a JavaScript runtime. Asciidoctor.js is used to power the AsciiDoc language preview extensions for Chrome, Atom, Brackets, and other web-based tooling."
                  }
                ],
                "roles": [],
                "title": null
              },
              {
                "context": "paragraph",
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "To be accurate, you can run Asciidoctor using Ruby, a JVM, or JavaScript. This documentation focuses on the Ruby version of Asciidoctor."
                  }
                ],
                "roles": [],
                "title": null
              }
            ],
            "context": "section",
            "id": "_javascript",
            "level": 2,
            "roles": [],
            "title": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "JavaScript"
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
